---
/**
 * The support chat: one persistent launcher and one pinned drawer, on every page.
 *
 * REBUILT 2026-08-18 after Oleh saw the first version. That one lived in the docs rail and pushed
 * the reading column from 736 to 600px when it opened. I recommended that shape and he picked it
 * from my recommendation; seeing it, he reversed — the page "стрибає", and it does: opening the
 * chat re-centred the main pane, swapped the rail's contents and moved every line of text. Three
 * corrections came out of it, and all three follow from ONE decision:
 *
 * **The chat does not belong to the page. It belongs to the visitor.**
 *
 *   1. It takes part in NO layout. `position: fixed`, so nothing reflows, ever — opening and
 *      closing move exactly zero pixels of the document. At 1440 the drawer sits over the table of
 *      contents, not over the prose (text ends ~920px, the drawer starts ~1025).
 *   2. It ANIMATES in and out, because a panel that belongs to the visitor should look like it
 *      arrived rather than like the page changed. `prefers-reduced-motion` turns it off.
 *   3. Its header does NOT say which page you are reading. The old "Reading: Retention and cleanup"
 *      re-titled itself on every navigation, which made the panel read as a property of the page —
 *      the exact opposite of a conversation you carry with you.
 *
 * WHAT THIS DOES NOT THROW AWAY. Dan asked for "context-aware chat", and the context is still
 * captured: every question is stored with the page it was asked from (`ctx` in `chat-core.ts`), so
 * the engine can ground its answer. The change is that the context travels with the QUESTION and no
 * longer brands the CHROME. Oleh's model — the visitor browses the docs on the left or talks to the
 * chat on the right, and the chat answers with links they can follow while it stays put — is served
 * by that, and the old header was actively working against it.
 *
 * THE LAUNCHER IS NOT HERE ANY MORE. It sat bottom-right as a floating bubble; it now lives in the
 * header beside Search (`components/Search.astro`), Oleh's call from Stripe's docs — asking is the
 * same job as searching, one step further along, so the two belong in one pair rather than having a
 * permanent floating object compete with the page. This file still owns the drawer, and the header
 * button targets it with the same `data-chat-open` hook.
 *
 * THE PAGE CONTEXT IS A CHIP, NOT A TITLE. v2 dropped "Reading: <page>" from the header because a
 * subtitle that renames itself on every navigation makes the panel read as a property of the page.
 * Stripe's pattern resolves that properly and is what is built here: the page is a REMOVABLE CHIP
 * above the composer. Keep it and the question is scoped to what you are reading; dismiss it and
 * the scope is the whole corpus, which is exactly when starter questions are worth offering. So
 * Dan's "context-aware" and Oleh's "independent of the page" stop being in tension — the context
 * became something the visitor can see and take off.
 *
 * WHY IT IS RENDERED FROM THE BANNER. `Banner` is the one component Starlight renders on EVERY page
 * (`Page.astro` puts it above both the hero branch and the article branch), so this is the only
 * override that reaches the landing, the docs and the custom pages alike without duplicating it.
 *
 * NOTHING ABOVE MAY BECOME A CONTAINING BLOCK. `position: fixed` escapes ancestor overflow, but a
 * `transform`, `filter` or `container-type` on any ancestor would capture it and pin this drawer to
 * that element instead of the viewport. `SupportHero.astro` needed a full-bleed and originally took
 * `container-type: inline-size` on `main` to get it — which would have trapped this. It uses
 * `overflow-x: clip` instead, for exactly this reason.
 */
import { CHAT_STARTERS } from '../lib/questions';
import Icon from './Icon.astro';

import PlatformPicker from './PlatformPicker.astro';

const FREE_BUDGET = 5;
const route = Astro.locals.starlightRoute;
/* The splash landing is not "about" a page in any useful sense, so it gets no chip and the
   starters show instead — the same state a visitor reaches by dismissing the chip elsewhere. */
const pageTitle = route.entry.data.template === 'splash' ? '' : route.entry.data.title;
---

<aside
  class="cd"
  data-pagefind-ignore
  data-chat-panel
  data-budget={FREE_BUDGET}
  data-context={pageTitle}
  role="dialog"
  aria-label="Support chat"
  aria-modal="false"
  hidden
>
  <div
    class="cd-grip"
    data-chat-grip
    role="separator"
    aria-orientation="vertical"
    aria-label="Resize chat"
    tabindex="0"
  ></div>

  <header class="cd-head">
    <h2 class="cd-title">Support chat</h2>
    <button type="button" class="cd-close" data-chat-close aria-label="Close chat">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M18 6 6 18" /><path d="m6 6 12 12" /></svg>
    </button>
  </header>

  <div class="cd-messages" data-chat-messages aria-live="polite"></div>

  <div class="cd-starters" data-chat-starters hidden>
    {CHAT_STARTERS.map((q) => (
      <button type="button" class="cd-starter" data-chat-starter>{q}</button>
    ))}
  </div>

  {pageTitle && (
    <div class="cd-chip" data-chat-chip>
      <span class="cd-chip-icon" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M15 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V7Z"/><path d="M14 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h4"/></svg>
      </span>
      <span class="cd-chip-label">{pageTitle}</span>
      <button type="button" class="cd-chip-x" data-chat-chip-clear aria-label="Ask about all of the docs instead">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="12" height="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M18 6 6 18"/><path d="m6 6 12 12"/></svg>
      </button>
    </div>
  )}

  {/* THE SAME SCOPE THE DOCS AND THE SEARCH USE (Oleh, 2026-08-20), and now the same control.
      The assistant answers OUT of these pages, so a reader who has narrowed the documentation to
      Notion and then gets an answer built from Airtable's pages has been given a wrong answer, not
      an inconsistent one. It belongs beside the page chip because that row is already where this
      conversation's scope is declared and taken away.

      NO `chipsUpTo`, AND THIS SURFACE IS WHY THE PROP WAS NEVER RIGHT. The rail opens at 368px and
      the drag handle takes it to 320px, so the room here MOVES while a build-time count cannot. It
      declared 2 and collapsed at every width, which was the correct answer arrived at by guessing
      the layout. Since 2026-08-21 the disclosure is the only presentation, so nothing here is
      decided by a number anybody has to keep true.

      `drop="up"` — this sits one row above the composer at the foot of a full-height drawer, and a
      list opening downwards would run off the bottom of the viewport with its last options on it. */}
  <div class="cd-plat">
    <PlatformPicker label="Answers from" drop="up" />
  </div>

  <form class="cd-composer" data-chat-form>
    <input type="text" data-chat-input placeholder="Ask a question…" autocomplete="off" required aria-label="Ask a question" />
    <button type="submit" aria-label="Send">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m5 12 7-7 7 7" /><path d="M12 19V5" /></svg>
    </button>
  </form>

  {/* ── `Ask a person` → `Email support` (Oleh, from Dan's ruling, 2026-08-26) ────────────────────
      Dan: a reader will never be able to talk to a real person in real time. The CONTROL already
      obeyed that — it writes the transcript to `sessionStorage` and navigates to
      `/contact/?kind=ticket&from=chat`, so what happens is a ticket and an email, never a live
      conversation. The LABEL did not. "Ask a person" is present tense and reads as "start talking to
      someone now", which is exactly the promise that will not be kept, and PRODUCT.md's own voice
      rule is to state the limit before the reader hits it.

      `Email support` names the channel, so the wait is in the label rather than discovered after the
      click. It cannot be read as real-time by anyone. It is also the same 13 characters, so the foot
      row it shares with the message count does not move.

      THE BUTTON STAYS, and that is not a softening of the ruling — it is a different question from
      the one Dan answered. PRODUCT.md:26 makes reaching a human in one step half of what success
      means here, and principle 6 requires the path to be reachable from every page. Removing it
      would leave a reader whose answer was not in the documentation, or who has spent five messages,
      with no exit but to find `/contact/` themselves. Async is the limit; a dead end is not. */}
  <div class="cd-gate" data-chat-gate hidden>
    You have used your free messages. Email support below — it carries this conversation to the
    contact form, and a person replies by email.
  </div>

  <div class="cd-foot" data-chat-foot>
    {/* ── IT NAMES THE NEXT STEP, NOT THE COUNTER (Oleh, 2026-08-26) ────────────────────────────
        It read "Two answers in a row found nothing in the documentation.", and the question asked of
        it was the right one: what is this for, and how does it help. Three faults, and none of them
        is the mechanic.

        IT REPORTED THE SYSTEM'S BOOKKEEPING. "Two answers in a row" is `noCiteStreak` read aloud.
        The reader does not have a streak; the reader has a question nobody has answered. A count of
        the assistant's failures is the assistant's business.

        IT NAMED NO NEXT STEP. This line exists because the control beside it changes appearance at
        the same instant — ghost to soft teal — and a control that restyles itself in silence is
        worse than one that never moves. A status where a next step belongs leaves the reader to
        infer the connection.

        AND IT CLAIMED MORE THAN WE KNOW. "found nothing in the documentation" reads two ways: the
        documentation does not cover this, or the assistant failed to find it. Only the second is
        something this app can observe. PRODUCT.md is explicit that a limit is stated, not implied,
        and stating the wrong limit is not better than stating none.

        The wording now says what is true and what to do with it, in that order. `A person` rather
        than a repeat of the button's own label: the button says how, this says who. */}
    <p class="cd-why" data-chat-why hidden>
      The documentation has not answered this. A person can take it from here.
    </p>
    <div class="cd-foot-row">
      <p class="cd-left" data-chat-left aria-live="polite"></p>
      {/* The glyph carries the same half of the message as the word, for the reader who scans the
          row rather than reads it — the mail mark says "this ends in an inbox" before the label is
          read at all. Same Lucide set and the same 14px as every other inline glyph in this app. */}
      <button type="button" class="cd-esc" data-chat-escalate>
        <span class="cd-esc-ic" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="mail" size={14} /></span>Email support
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
</aside>

<script>
  import { wirePanel } from '../lib/chat-panel';
  wirePanel();
</script>

<style>
  /* ── Drawer ── */
  .cd {
    position: fixed;
    /* Above Starlight's menu layer (`--sl-z-index-menu`), which the expanded mobile nav uses. The
       drawer is portalled to `<body>` at hydration, so this number is finally measured against the
       page rather than against `.main-pane`'s isolated context. */
    z-index: 1000;
    inset-block: var(--sl-nav-height) 0;
    inset-inline-end: 0;
    /* 23rem = 368px, which is exactly the gap to the right of the reading column at 1440
       (content ends 1057, viewport 1425). At 25rem the drawer started at 1025 and lay over the
       last 32px of the content box — invisible in prose, which rarely fills the measure, but it
       would have clipped the right edge of every wide table and code block. Flush instead. */
    /* `--cd-w` is written by the drag handle and restored from storage; the literal is only the
       first-run default. 23rem = 368px is the gap right of the reading column at 1440. */
    width: var(--cd-w, min(23rem, 100vw));
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 16px;
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
    border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    box-shadow: -16px 0 40px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.24);
    /* Animated, so the panel reads as having ARRIVED rather than as the page having changed.
       `visibility` is in the transition because `hidden` cannot animate and a purely transparent
       panel would still swallow clicks on the way out. */
    transform: translateX(100%);
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    transition: transform 220ms cubic-bezier(0.32, 0.72, 0, 1), opacity 180ms ease,
      visibility 0s linear 220ms;
  }

  /* `[hidden]` is dropped by the script as soon as it runs; the flag drives the animation. Both are
     needed: the attribute keeps the panel out of the accessibility tree before hydration, the flag
     is what the transition listens to. */
  .cd[hidden] { display: none; }

  :global(html[data-chat='open']) .cd {
    transform: translateX(0);
    opacity: 1;
    visibility: visible;
    transition-delay: 0s;
  }

  /* Restoring an already-open drawer after a navigation must not replay the entry animation. */
  /* Restoring an already-open drawer must not replay the entry animation; and a drag must not be
     eased, or the edge visibly lags the cursor. */
  :global(html[data-chat-boot]) .cd,
  :global(html[data-chat-resizing]) .cd {
    transition: none;
  }

  /* While dragging, stop the pointer selecting prose it passes over. */
  :global(html[data-chat-resizing]) {
    user-select: none;
    cursor: col-resize;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .cd {
      transition-duration: 0s;
    }
  }

  /* ── Resize handle ──
     A 10px hit area straddling the drawer's leading edge; only its 1px centre is ever painted, and
     only on hover or focus, so the chrome does not advertise itself while nobody is aiming at it. */
  .cd-grip {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    inset-inline-start: -5px;
    width: 10px;
    cursor: col-resize;
    touch-action: none;
  }

  .cd-grip::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    inset-inline-start: 4px;
    width: 2px;
    background: var(--bo-primary);
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 120ms ease;
  }

  .cd-grip:hover::after,
  .cd-grip:focus-visible::after,
  :global(html[data-chat-resizing]) .cd-grip::after {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .cd-grip:focus-visible {
    outline: none;
  }

  .cd-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .cd-title {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--sl-text-base);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--sl-color-white);
  }

  .cd-close {
    display: flex;
    padding: 6px;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-6);
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .cd-close:hover { background: var(--bo-base-200); color: var(--sl-color-white); }

  .cd-messages {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
    padding-inline-end: 4px;
  }

  /* Built by `createElement` at runtime, so these MUST be global — a scoped rule never reaches a
     node the browser made after render. That is the defect which left every message a visitor ever
     sent unstyled on the old `/chat/` page for the whole life of that page. */
  .cd-messages :global(.msg) {
    max-width: 92%;
    padding: 8px 12px;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
    line-height: 1.5;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
  }

  .cd-messages :global(.msg.user) {
    align-self: flex-end;
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
    color: var(--bo-soft-primary-fg);
  }

  .cd-messages :global(.msg.bot) {
    align-self: flex-start;
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    color: var(--sl-color-text);
  }

  /* ── Cited docs ──
     A `<details>`, so the browser owns the toggle, its keyboard behaviour and its open state. The
     list is rebuilt from the stored turn on replay rather than kept alive as a component. */
  .cd-messages :global(.msg-sources) {
    margin-top: 8px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    padding-top: 8px;
    font-size: var(--sl-text-xs);
  }

  .cd-messages :global(.msg-sources > summary) {
    cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--sl-color-text-accent);
    list-style: none;
  }

  .cd-messages :global(.msg-sources > summary::-webkit-details-marker) { display: none; }

  .cd-messages :global(.msg-sources > summary::before) {
    content: '▸';
    display: inline-block;
    width: 12px;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .cd-messages :global(.msg-sources[open] > summary::before) { content: '▾'; }

  .cd-messages :global(.msg-sources ul) {
    margin: 6px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .cd-messages :global(.msg-sources a) {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    color: var(--sl-color-text-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .cd-messages :global(.msg-sources a:hover) { text-decoration: underline; }

  .cd-messages :global(.msg-sources svg) { flex: none; opacity: 0.7; }

  /* ── The platform scope ──
     The control is `PlatformPicker`; all that is left here is where it sits. `align-self` keeps it
     from stretching to the drawer's full width, and the margin reset is Starlight's prose rhythm,
     which margins every sibling after the first inside any flex box it can reach. `:global(*)` on
     the child half deliberately: the picker is another component and carries no scope hash of
     ours, so a bare `> *` would ship as valid CSS that selects nothing. */
  .cd-plat {
    align-self: flex-start;
    max-width: 100%;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .cd-plat > :global(*) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* ── The page chip ── */
  .cd-chip {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    align-self: flex-start;
    max-width: 100%;
    padding: 4px 4px 4px 8px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    font-size: var(--sl-text-xs);
    color: var(--sl-color-text);
  }

  .cd-chip[hidden] { display: none; }

  .cd-chip-icon { display: flex; flex: none; color: var(--bo-muted); }

  .cd-chip-label {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .cd-chip-x {
    display: flex;
    flex: none;
    padding: 3px;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .cd-chip-x:hover { background: var(--bo-base-300); color: var(--sl-color-white); }

  /* ── Starter questions ── */
  .cd-starters {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 6px;
  }

  .cd-starters[hidden] { display: none; }

  .cd-starter {
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    background: none;
    color: var(--sl-color-text-accent);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
    text-align: start;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .cd-starter:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

  .cd-composer { display: flex; gap: 8px; }

  .cd-composer input {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 8px 12px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    /* The field a person types into is a surface, not a well — `base-100`, which is white in the
       light theme. */
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
    color: var(--sl-color-text);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
  }

  .cd-composer input:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary); outline-offset: -1px; }

  .cd-composer button {
    flex: none;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 36px;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: var(--bo-primary);
    color: var(--bo-primary-content);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .cd-left { margin: 0; font-size: var(--sl-text-xs); color: var(--bo-muted); }

  /* ── The exit to a person ──
     THE PLACEMENT IS THE DECISION. It is one row under the input, beside the count, which is where
     Base44 pins its escalate and where this drawer already says the one other thing that is true
     of the conversation rather than of a message. Inside the composer it would have sat next to
     Send at the same weight and competed with it; on its own line it is present from the first
     turn without ever being the thing you reach for first.

     AND IT IS OUTSIDE THE FORM, so the spent-budget gate — which hides the composer — cannot take
     the escape hatch with it. That is also why the gate no longer prints a link of its own: two
     doors out, eight pixels apart, is one door too many. */
  .cd-foot {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .cd-foot-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 8px;
    /* Starlight's prose rhythm margins every sibling after the first inside any flex box it can
       reach, and the count paragraph is one. */
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .cd-foot-row > .cd-left { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }

  /* Tertiary at rest: the label carries it, and a glyph beside two plain words would be the third
     icon in a 368px column saying nothing the words do not. */
  .cd-esc {
    flex: none;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    padding: 6px 8px;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--sl-color-text-accent);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--sl-text-xs);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background-color 120ms ease, border-color 120ms ease;
  }

  .cd-esc-ic { display: inline-flex; flex: none; opacity: 0.8; }

  .cd-esc:hover { background: var(--bo-base-200); }
  .cd-esc:hover .cd-esc-ic { opacity: 1; }

  .cd-esc:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary); outline-offset: 1px; }

  /* PROMOTED IN PLACE — the same control, more weight. Two consecutive answers that cited nothing
     is the evidence the documentation does not hold this, and the soft primary pair is the loudest
     step this app takes without becoming a call to action. No popup: the moment somebody has been
     failed twice is the worst moment to put a layer over what they were reading. */
  .cd-foot[data-promoted='1'] .cd-esc {
    border-color: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
    color: var(--bo-soft-primary-fg);
  }

  .cd-foot[data-promoted='1'] .cd-esc:hover { border-color: var(--bo-primary); }

  .cd-why {
    margin-top: 0;
    font-size: var(--sl-text-xs);
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* An author `display` beats the `hidden` attribute, and this file has been bitten by that. */
  .cd-why[hidden] { display: none; }

  .cd-gate {
    padding: 12px;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
    color: var(--sl-color-text);
  }

  .cd-gate[hidden] { display: none; }

  /* ── ON A PHONE THE DRAWER IS THE SCREEN, AND NOW IT ACTUALLY IS (2026-08-25) ─────────────────
     This block's comment already said "there is nothing to sit beside" and then moved only the top
     edge. Measured at an emulated 390x844: the panel opened at **x=22, 368 wide** — 94% of the
     screen, with a 22px ribbon of the page still showing down the left, carrying the left half of
     whatever letters the article had there. It read as a rendering fault rather than as a panel.

     The 368 was never a phone number. `.cd`'s own comment derives 23rem from the gap to the right
     of the reading column at 1440, which is the correct desktop value and meaningless here; the
     `min(…, 100vw)` cap could only ever have produced a full-bleed sheet at widths under 368, i.e.
     never on a real phone.

     `--cd-w` IS OVERRIDDEN, NOT JUST DEFAULTED. It is written by the drag handle and restored from
     storage, so a reader who widened the drawer on a desktop arrives here carrying that number —
     and a stored 600px would have painted 600px of drawer into a 390px screen. `width: 100%` in the
     declaration rather than a wider fallback is what makes the stored value unreachable at this
     width instead of merely unlikely.

